  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 221 AM MST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nNorthwesterly flow continues to be favored overhead Sunday \nmorning, as troughing exits the area to the east. NBM and LREF \nguidance are consistent that precipitation with this system will\nbe out of the area before sunrise. A 60-80 kt jet streak at the\nbase of the trough will likely support a surface high in its \nleft entrance region. GEFS and EC members are in good agreement \nthat this high will be entering the area from the northwest, \nproviding northwesterly surface flow through the morning and \nparts of the afternoon. High temperatures are forecast in the \n40s across the area Sunday, with lows in the 20s. No hazardous \nweather is expected at this time. \n\nBy Monday morning, upper flow is forecast to be more zonal \n(west to east), lasting through Tuesday with only a few weak \ndisturbances. Ensembles indicate a shortwave trough developing \nover the area Monday, but precipitation is not favored save for \n14% of LREF solutions which show a deeper shortwave. However, \neven these scenarios give less than a 30% chance that \nprecipitation occurs at all across the Tri-State Area. As this \nsystem exits, a weak embedded ridge is forecast overhead \nTuesday. Overall, winds are likely to remain light, with highs \nmainly in the 50s both days. \n\nEnsemble guidance shows troughing across the west Wednesday \nmorning, moving eastward into the forecast area throughout the \nday. GEFS and EC members support a low just to the west of the \nforecast area to start this period, though it is not certain how\nintense it will be. Some form of southerly flow may be in place\nduring the day, with current forecast highs in the 50s. \nHowever, a cold front is expected with this system, and timing \nis a bit uncertain. Wednesday's highs could be a bit lower in \nthe 40s with a quicker passage, or higher in the 60s if passage \nis slower. Highs on Thursday behind the front are forecast in \nthe 40s. NBM 12 hr precipitation guidance shows precipitation is\nmost likely during the evening and night hours Wednesday, \nthough still only at around a 20% chance of occurring. This may \nbe in part due to a dry airmass in place at the time, which can \nbe seen looking at the 300 K Theta-e surface. Relative humidity \nduring the day is shown below 60%, with dewpoints mainly less \nthan -5 C. Relative humidities increase going into the night \nalong this surface, which may correspond to the higher \nprecipitation chances. LREF clusters show precipitation is most \nlikely in the event of a deeper trough, with possibly some \nwintry precipitation mixed in. Guidance indicates any \nprecipitation should be moved out of the area by midday \nThursday.",
